1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:03,689
When part of an outbreak map is blank,

2
00:00:03,739 --> 00:00:04,807
what does it mean?

3
00:00:05,857 --> 00:00:09,418
Were zero cases reported,
or were no data available?

4
00:00:10,068 --> 00:00:12,133
These are two very different situations.

5
00:00:12,433 --> 00:00:15,095
Neither proves there was no transmission.

6
00:00:17,245 --> 00:00:21,663
In March 2020, as COVID-19
emerged across sub-Saharan Africa,

7
00:00:21,713 --> 00:00:25,873
my colleagues and I examined reported
outbreak patterns in 39 countries.

8
00:00:26,223 --> 00:00:29,793
We wanted to understand what the
available evidence could tell us during

9
00:00:29,843 --> 00:00:32,123
a period of substantial uncertainty.

10
00:00:33,572 --> 00:00:36,906
We combined COVID-19 surveillance
data compiled by Johns Hopkins

11
00:00:36,956 --> 00:00:40,606
University with publicly available
health and demographic indicators

12
00:00:40,656 --> 00:00:45,604
from the World Bank,
WHO, UNICEF, and UNAIDS.

13
00:00:47,414 --> 00:00:50,164
Integration gave us a broader
view of the available evidence.

14
00:00:52,014 --> 00:00:55,326
We used Lasso-based methods to
examine candidate variables associated

15
00:00:55,376 --> 00:00:57,238
with reported case counts.

16
00:00:57,927 --> 00:01:00,767
Seven variables were statistically
associated in the inferential

17
00:01:00,817 --> 00:01:02,046
analysis.

18
00:01:02,696 --> 00:01:05,947
These relationships were exploratory
and should not be interpreted as

19
00:01:05,997 --> 00:01:07,394
proof of causation.

20
00:01:09,444 --> 00:01:13,062
Empirical Bayesian Kriging uses
spatial relationships among observed

21
00:01:13,112 --> 00:01:16,712
measurements to estimate patterns
between those observations.

22
00:01:18,662 --> 00:01:23,052
Through repeated simulations, it
also accounts for uncertainty in the

23
00:01:23,102 --> 00:01:25,207
fitted spatial correlation model.

24
00:01:27,357 --> 00:01:29,398
The result is an estimated surface,

25
00:01:29,448 --> 00:01:33,108
not a record of infections in
locations without observations.

26
00:01:36,858 --> 00:01:40,778
Our spatial maps helped us explore
patterns in the available data.

27
00:01:41,128 --> 00:01:44,834
But they could not tell us where
infections had gone undetected.

28
00:01:45,384 --> 00:01:48,683
An estimated hotspot is
not a confirmed outbreak.

29
00:01:50,933 --> 00:01:54,191
Consider Ebola preparedness in the
Democratic Republic of the Congo and

30
00:01:54,241 --> 00:01:55,512
neighboring areas.

31
00:01:55,962 --> 00:01:58,940
Could spatial estimates, combined
with reliable surveillance,

32
00:01:58,990 --> 00:02:01,720
population, mobility,
and testing information,

33
00:02:01,770 --> 00:02:05,012
help teams decide where to
investigate more closely?

34
00:02:05,562 --> 00:02:06,489
Potentially.

35
00:02:06,939 --> 00:02:09,678
But the estimates would need
validation and local epidemiological

36
00:02:09,728 --> 00:02:12,285
interpretation before informing action.

37
00:02:14,435 --> 00:02:17,782
The goal is not to fill
every blank on the map.

38
00:02:18,082 --> 00:02:23,054
It is to use the evidence we have
to decide where to look next,

39
00:02:23,104 --> 00:02:26,470
without mistaking an
estimate for an observation.

40
00:02:28,320 --> 00:02:29,530
Follow Dr.

41
00:02:29,580 --> 00:02:32,012
Amobi Andrew Onovo for more.

42
00:02:32,212 --> 00:02:35,086
EvidenceLab. See it.

43
00:02:35,136 --> 00:02:37,933
Understand it. Run it.
